MASTER (Method of Accelerated Search for Tertiary Ensemble Representatives)
is an open-source C++ program for performing structural-similarity searches.
Copyright (C) 2014 Jianfu Zhou, Gevorg Grigoryan

Web site: http://www.grigoryanlab.org/master/

If used in a scientific publication, please cite: Zhou, Grigoryan (2015), 
"Rapid search for tertiary fragments reveals protein sequence–structure 
relationships". Protein Science, 24: 508–524. doi: 10.1002/pro.2610

Contact:
Gevorg Grigoryan
gevorg.grigoryan@dartmouth.edu
Dartmouth College


==== A quick usage guide ====

To use MASTER you need a query and a database. Both of these are 
created using the createPDS tool. PDS stands for ?Protein Data 
Structure? and, as the name suggests, this tool takes as input a PDB 
structure and produces a file representing it that will later be used 
as either a query or one of the database entries in a MASTER search. 
Converting PDB files into PDS files enables MASTER to search rapidly 
by representing the structure in a manner amenable to fast lookups 
(see Zhou and Grigoryan, 2015). Here are some example commands:

<path>/createPDS --type query --pdb myQuery.pdb --pds myQuery.pds
<path>/createPDS --type target --pdb struct123.pdb --pds /my/database/str123.pds

The first command prepares a query entry, myQuery.pds, whereas the 
second command processes structure struct123.pdb to be one of the 
database entries, placing it into some appropriate location on the 
file system (/my/database/str123.pds). The second command would be 
repeated for all structures making up the database, with a list of 
all corresponding PDS files (i.e., their full paths) compiled into a 
single list file to be used in searching. Execute createPDS without 
any options to see the list of all options?many features are 
available, including processing lists of files and breaking up large 
PDB structures with internal redundancies (e.g., virus structures) 
into their minimal units that preserve all unique chains and unique 
interfaces.

Once a query and a database (with its list file) are ready, MASTER 
can be used to perform the search. For example:

<path>/master --query myQuery.pds --targetList database.list 
--rmsdCut 1.6 --bbRMSD --matchOut myQuery.match 
--seqOut myQuery.seq --structOut myQuery.struct 
--outType match

This will search the database listed in database.list for all 
substructures within 1.6 A full-backbone RMSD (because --bbRMSD 
was specified) of the query in myQuery.pds. Note that the query 
may have one or more disjoint segments. Information on the matches 
(i.e., which database entry they come from and what indices each 
disjoint segment in the query maps to) is output into myQuery.match, 
one match per line. Sequences of the matching regions, in the order 
of residues in the query, are output into myQuery.seq, one match per 
line. The full-backbone RMSD of each match is also listed in this 
file in the first column. Finally, structures of matching regions 
are written into myQuery.struct/ directory, one file per match (full 
matches can also be output). Execute master by itself for the full 
list of options.
